course: dictionary
lesson: 10001
title: Looking far away with a telescope
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A telescope is a tube with lenses or mirrors inside. It makes faraway things, like stars and planets, look bigger and closer. «In 1609-1610 Galileo's homemade telescope showed moons going round Jupiter.» Jupiter is very far away, but the telescope brought its moons closer to Galileo's eyes.
explain: Look for clue words in the sentence. «Venus is wrapped in thick clouds of sulphuric acid that hide its surface completely from ordinary telescopes.» The word hide tells you the clouds block the view, so an ordinary telescope cannot see the surface. Scientists used radar, a different tool, to map it.
explain: Some words in science have a special meaning. «A planetary nebula has nothing to do with planets.» The name came from old telescopes, where a cloud of gas looked like a round planet. The context warns you not to guess from the word planet alone.
fact: A telescope makes far-away things look bigger and closer.
fact: «Telescope» is a noun, and you can say «look through a telescope» or «a space telescope».
fact: Galileo's telescope, around 1609, showed moons going round Jupiter.
fact: «Air blurs and blocks some light», so big telescopes sit on high, dry mountains.
fact: Some telescopes, like the Hubble and James Webb telescopes, are in space.
exercise: What does a telescope do? | Makes near things, like your shoes, look smaller | *Makes far-away things, like stars and planets, look bigger and closer | Measures how fast the wind blows
exercise: «Venus is wrapped in thick clouds that hide its surface completely from ordinary telescopes.» What does hide suggest? | Moves something faster | Shows you something clearly | Makes something bigger | *Stops you from seeing something
exercise: Why do big telescopes sit on high, dry mountains? | Because mountains are quieter for sleeping | To be closer to the stars | *Because air blurs and blocks some light | To get a better view of the sea
test: Which telescope is in space? | *The James Webb telescope | A pair of binoculars | A bicycle | A telescope lens in a shop
test: Why is a planetary nebula called that? | It is a planet with rings | *It looked like a round planet in old telescopes | It is made of planets | It was found on a planet's surface
exam: What did astronauts say about the Great Wall? | They were not looking at it at all | They saw it clearly through a pair of binoculars | *They could not see it with the naked eye from orbit | They could see every stone with the naked eye
